{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787082995,"nonce":"PX7OafxUnqPTSYapB434Y69hrh0=","title":"`WindowOrdinal` returns 0 for the window that starts at the Unix epoch, and `notifications_sver_ck` refuses a `state_version` below 1","message":"**What is wrong.** A digest Notification carries the window's ordinal in `state_version`, and `notifications_sver_ck CHECK (state_version \u003e= 1)` has stood on that column since `00011`. `WindowOrdinal` is integer division of the window start's Unix seconds by the window length in seconds, so the window that STARTS at the epoch yields 0. `DigestWindows` can return that window. The insert then fails with a `23514` naming a constraint whose invariant the caller believed it satisfied, and the code comment asserting the invariant is where the mistake is written down.\n\n**The demonstration.** `internal/notification/domain/digest.go:137-142`:\n\n```go\nfunc (d Digest) WindowOrdinal(start time.Time) int {\n\tif !d.Enabled() {\n\t\treturn 0\n\t}\n\treturn int(start.UTC().Unix() / int64(d.Window/time.Second))\n}\n```\n\nAt `start = time.Unix(0, 0)` the numerator is 0 and the result is 0 for every window length. The comment four lines above (`:128-136`) states the bound it is claiming: *\"it satisfies notifications_sver_ck (\u003e= 1) for every window after the first of 1970.\"* The qualifier is the defect — \"after the first\" is the arithmetic's own escape clause, written down and then not enforced.\n\nThe epoch window is reachable from `DigestWindows`, not merely constructible by hand. `:193-196` computes `newest := d.WindowStart(now).Add(-d.Window)` and returns early only when `newest.Before(time.Unix(0, 0))` — *before*, not *at or before*. So when `now` falls in the SECOND window since the epoch, `WindowStart(now)` is `W`, `newest` is exactly `time.Unix(0,0)`, the guard does not fire, and `:198-200` returns `[]time.Time{epoch}` for a policy that has never digested. `sweepPolicy` then folds that window and `emit` builds the intent with `StateVersion: p.Digest.WindowOrdinal(start)` (`internal/notification/service/digest.go`, the `emit` construction), which is 0.\n\nThe database is the only thing that catches it: `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:227` is `CONSTRAINT notifications_sver_ck CHECK (state_version \u003e= 1)`. The failure is a `23514` from the insert, not a validation error with a field name, and it fails the whole digest tick for that policy rather than that window.\n\n**Why it matters.** In production this is unreachable: `now` would have to be inside the second window after 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z. The honest reading is that this is a test-clock defect and an invariant defect, not an outage. It is worth a row anyway for two reasons. First, oto's clock is injected everywhere precisely so tests can put it wherever they like, and `internal/notification/domain/digest_test.go:432` already exercises `WindowOrdinal` at the zero value — so the next person who writes a digest test starting from `time.Unix(0,0)`, which is the obvious thing to reach for, gets a `23514` from a CHECK constraint and no clue that the ordinal is the cause. Second, a bound that arithmetic can violate is a bound the arithmetic should assert. The rest of this file is careful about exactly this: `WindowStart` (`:115-121`) handles pre-1970 instants with an explicit floor-division correction under a comment saying *\"a boundary function that is wrong for half the number line is a trap for whoever first writes a test with a zero time.\"* `WindowOrdinal` is the same function's sibling and does not get the same care.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/domain/digest.go:128-142` (`WindowOrdinal` and the comment stating the bound it does not hold) · `:187-200` (`DigestWindows`, whose `Before` guard admits the epoch window) · `:109-123` (`WindowStart`, the sibling that does handle the edge) · `internal/notification/service/digest.go` `emit` (`StateVersion: p.Digest.WindowOrdinal(start)`) · `db/migrations/00011_notification.sql:227` (`notifications_sver_ck`) · `db/migrations/00058_a_digest_is_a_window_over_a_namespace.sql:114` (which repeats the same \"for every window after 1970\" qualifier) · `internal/notification/domain/digest_test.go:405-435` (which asserts `\u003e= 1` for a mid-range window and `0` for a disabled digest, and covers neither of the two epoch cases).\n\n**Scope.** small.\n\n**Done when.** No input to `WindowOrdinal` that `DigestWindows` can produce yields a value below 1 — either the ordinal is 1-based, or `DigestWindows` excludes the epoch window with `!newest.After(time.Unix(0,0))`, or the ordinal is clamped where the intent is built. A test asserts the bound over the epoch window rather than over a comfortable timestamp, and the comment at `:128-136` states the invariant without the \"after 1970\" qualifier — or states the exclusion that makes the qualifier unnecessary. Changing the ordinal's base is a change to `state_version` on stored digest rows; if that road is taken, the §C.7 idempotency key it feeds is checked for whether an off-by-one re-mints an intent for a window already sent.","files":null}]}